<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Septinsular Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1807 CE · capital Corfu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Republic of the Seven Islands under Russian and Ottoman protection, the first self-governing Greek state of modern times.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1798-1799 a joint Russian-Ottoman expedition under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-ushakov">Fyodor Ushakov</a> drove the French from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-of-the-ionian-islands">Ionian Islands</a>. The Treaty of Constantinople in 1800 created the Septinsular Republic of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paxos">Paxos</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leucas">Lefkada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kythira">Kythira</a>, under Ottoman suzerainty and Russian protection.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was governed by a senate in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a> dominated by the island nobility, under a series of constitutions; that of 1803 widened participation. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spyridon-georgios-theotokis">Spyridon Theotokis</a> presided over the senate, and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a>, later the first governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, served as secretary of state. Greek became an official language alongside Italian.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Tilsit</a> in 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> ceded the islands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and they returned to French rule as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-ionian-islands">French Ionian Islands</a>. The republic's experience of self-rule fed the ideas of the Greek national movement.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/septinsular-republic.jpg" alt="Septinsular Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Septinsular_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1800 CE – 1807 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Corfu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Spyridon Theotokis, Ioannis Kapodistrias</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Seven Islands, Septinsular Republic of the Ionian Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=septinsular-republic" aria-label="Septinsular Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5172 759 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Septinsular Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5492 1015L5492 1015M5492 1015L5709 970M5492 1015L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5492 1015h0M5709 970h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5492 1015h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5492 1015h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Corfu. <a href="/atlas?year=1804&amp;focus=septinsular-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
